# This host's default MacOSX27.2 SDK ships a libpthread.tbd the linker
# cannot parse ("unknown architecture"); the 26.5 SDK links cleanly.
# Use it when present; harmless elsewhere.
MAC_SDK := $(shell if [ -d /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX26.5.sdk ]; then echo /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX26.5.sdk; fi)
DUNE_BUILD = $(if $(MAC_SDK),SDKROOT=$(MAC_SDK) ,)dune build$(if $(findstring /.bp3/worktrees/,$(CURDIR)), --root=.,)

check: lint
	./scripts/check-skills skills .pi/skills .agents/skills .claude/skills
	./test/skills.sh
	$(DUNE_BUILD) src/quest/main.exe
	./test/q.sh
	./test/ready.sh
	./test/diagram.sh
	./test/blocked.sh
	node test/pi-tree.test.mjs
	./test/harness.sh
	./test/config.sh
	./test/link.sh
	./test/dogfood-link.sh
	./test/dogfood-parallel.sh
	./test/context.sh
	node --test extensions/bp3-pi/test/bp3-pi.test.mjs
	./test/worktree.sh
	./test/tape.sh
	./test/hooks.sh
	./test/traverse.sh
	./test/completion.sh
	./test/release.sh
	./test/install.sh

lint:
	shellcheck bin/bp3 scripts/check-skills test/skills.sh test/q.sh test/ready.sh test/diagram.sh test/blocked.sh test/harness.sh test/config.sh test/link.sh test/dogfood-link.sh test/dogfood-parallel.sh test/context.sh test/worktree.sh test/tape.sh test/hooks.sh test/traverse.sh test/completion.sh test/release.sh test/install.sh completions/bp3.bash scripts/release-check scripts/install-bp3.sh scripts/bp3-shim scripts/release-publish
	node --check test/pi-tree.test.mjs
	node --check skills/traverse/workflows/induct-node.js
	node --check skills/traverse/workflows/node-loop.js
	node --check skills/traverse/workflows/induct-grade.js
	node --check skills/traverse/workflows/quest-land.js

test:
	./test/skills.sh

# --- release (Exp 5): tag-gated artifact + checksum; publish-once via gh ---
RELEASE_VERSION := $(shell sed -n 's/.*bp3_version = "\(.*\)".*/\1/p' src/quest/gitx.ml | head -1)
RELEASE_OS := $(shell uname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
RELEASE_ARCH := $(shell uname -m)
RELEASE_ARTIFACT := dist/bp3-q-v$(RELEASE_VERSION)-$(RELEASE_OS)-$(RELEASE_ARCH)

release:
	@./scripts/release-check >/dev/null
	$(DUNE_BUILD) src/quest/main.exe
	mkdir -p dist
	cp _build/default/src/quest/main.exe $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT)
	cd dist && shasum -a 256 $$(basename $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT)) > $$(basename $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT)).sha256
	@echo "released $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT)"

publish: release
	./scripts/release-publish $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT) $(RELEASE_ARTIFACT).sha256

PREFIX ?= $(HOME)/.local
install:
	PREFIX="$(PREFIX)" ./scripts/install-bp3.sh
===== skills/add-bp3-skill/SKILL.md =====
---
name: add-bp3-skill
description: Add a bp3 project skill under skills/ and install it per project with bp3 skills install, through overlap, statelessness, and trigger checks.
---

# Add BP3 Skill

Use when the user invokes this skill by name (`/add-bp3-skill`) or asks
to add a skill to bp3 itself. Project skills live in this repo, load only inside bp3, and
version with the code. This skill is the bp3-side gatekeeper; it writes
nothing until the checks pass.

Use add-yap-skill instead when the skill
is portable, stateless, and human-invoked across projects. If it could
work unchanged in esc3 or any other repo, it is a yap skill wearing bp3
clothes — route it there.

## Procedure

1. Clarify the job in one sentence: who invokes it (human, Controller,
   or Worker), in which repo context, and the one output shape it
   produces. Three example invocations and three near-misses that must
   not trigger it. No examples, no write.
2. Check overlap. Search bp3's `skills/`, the MVP doc, and the yap
   roster. If an existing skill absorbs the job with a section added,
   propose that edit instead. If the job is portable and stateless,
   stop and route to add-yap-skill.
3. Run the stateless test in reverse: project skills may touch bp3
   concepts (Quest files, Board ops, lanes), but the skill file itself
   stays instructions — no state, no hooks, no daemon assumptions.
   Anything needing runtime behavior belongs in core, not a skill.
4. Red chair, proportionate: wrong layer (core vs skill vs yap),
   context tax on Controller/Worker sessions that will load it, name
   collisions with yap skills across harnesses, and trigger overlap
   with quest/add-yap-skill.
5. Write the source at `skills/<skill-id>/SKILL.md`: frontmatter `name`
   equal to the directory, one-sentence `description`, no template placeholders, concise self-contained
   body in the house shape (when to use, procedure, rules).
6. Install and verify: run `./bin/bp3 skills install --harness=all`,
   then `muse skills validate .agents/skills/<skill-id> --json` must

===== skills/add-yap-skill/SKILL.md =====
---
name: add-yap-skill
description: "Add a portable, human-invoked skill to bp3-yap-skills through its gates: clarify, prior art, red chair, and trigger tests."
---

# Add Yap Skill

Use when the user invokes this skill by name (`/add-yap-skill`) or asks
to add a skill to bp3-yap-skills. This is the yap roster's gatekeeper, renamed so it can
live beside bp3 project skills without colliding with any global
`add-skill`. It writes nothing until every gate passes; failing a gate
produces a report, not a directory. Absorb-or-reject is the common
outcome.

Target repo: bp3-yap-skills (the personal, portable skill library).
Anything needing Board state, tool blocking, hooks, or runtime code does
not belong there — route it to an add-bp3-skill proposal instead.

## Gates, in order

1. Clarify. Use iiuc/ask discipline. The candidate gets: a one-sentence
   job, a human invoker by name, three example invocations, and three
   near-miss invocations that must not trigger it. No examples, no
   further gates. A candidate with no papercut, session, or repeated
   correction behind it stops here.
2. Search prior art. In order: the yap roster, bp2's catalog, bp1's
   workflow skills, then the internet (Superpowers, Compound
   Engineering, gstack, Anthropic's skills, a web search for the job in
   words). Output: a list of existing skill, overlap, absorb-or-not. If
   an existing skill absorbs it with a section added, the result is that
   edit, not a new skill.
3. Mine the logs (optional). When an archive miner exists, ask it for
   sessions for and against the candidate. Every claim needs session
   links and counts. No archive means this gate is `unchecked`, recorded
   as such — never as support, never as a blocker.
4. Red chair. Read-only adversarial pass: overlap stated plainly; the
   stateless test (any Board, queue, hook, or daemon need routes it out
   of yap, by name); failure modes; context tax on every future session;
   name collisions across harnesses. Verdict: absorb, reject, or proceed.
5. Adversarial checks. The three examples must trigger the candidate,

===== skills/icebox/SKILL.md =====
---
name: icebox
description: Freeze a not-yet-ready idea as a future-quest sketch in docs/icebox with today's context, the smallest workable shape, blockers, and a promotion trigger.
---

# Icebox

Use when a human in bp3 says to icebox, freeze, or park an idea
for a future quest (`/icebox`), or when a design session settles
a direction but deliberately defers it past the current quest.
One output: one entry in `docs/icebox/` that a later `/quest`
can promote by transcription.

Use `quest` instead when the idea is ready to frame now.
Use `add-bp3-skill` or `add-yap-skill` when the idea is a skill.
Use `red-ink` when an existing design needs a decision walk.

## Procedure

1. Capture today's context first: what was being designed, the
   decision just settled or deferred, and links to the files
   that hold it. Context is written for a reader who was not
   in the session.
2. Sketch the smallest version that would work: states, edges,
   commands, or files it touches — and what it deliberately
   does not add.
3. List every blocker already known: missing facts, undecided
   questions, dependencies, identity/data gaps, tests that
   would fail today. “None known” is a claim; make it honestly.
4. Name the promotion trigger: the observable signal that says
   thaw this now. No trigger, no entry — an idea that can never
   ripen is a note, not an icebox entry.
5. Write `docs/icebox/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` (today's date,
   short kebab slug, chosen with `naming` if the slug is not
   obvious) with frontmatter `status: frozen`, `date`, and
   `promoted: null`, and sections Context, Sketch, Blockers,
   Promotion trigger, matching `docs/icebox/README.md`.
6. If the idea was deferred inside a live design, record the
   deferral where the design lives (one line: deferred to the
   icebox entry, with its path). The design keeps no shadow

===== skills/naming/SKILL.md =====
---
name: naming
description: "Choose concrete, short names in bp3's existing vocabulary for quests, exps, skills, plans, engines, and CLI verbs."
---

# Naming

Use when a human, Controller, or Worker in bp3 must name or
rename something: a quest slug, exp, skill, plan (`quest_type`),
engine, CLI verb, file, or state. Invoked by name (`/naming`,
`$naming`). One output: one recommended name, with the vocabulary
it reuses. A shortlist only when two names genuinely tie.

Use `quest` for framing a quest (it delegates naming here),
`using-bp3` for operating the CLI, and general judgment for
ordinary code identifiers with no bp3 meaning.

## The vocabulary (reuse before inventing)

- Planning: Quest, exp, Run. Lifecycle: proposed, active,
  landed, canceled. Never archived, cancelled, aborted, killed.
- Moves: handoff, grade, verify, land, block, resume. Stages: author,
  grade, fix, ready, landed, blocked, canceled.
- Dimensions: `quest_type` (the plan: default is implemented;
  design, mvp, refactor, frontend, backend are proposed), `engine` (who executes: single,
  master-subagents, panes, workflows).
- Artifacts: QUEST.md, fsm.yaml, state.yaml, ledger.jsonl,
  tape.log, notes.md, experiments/, runs/.
- Ceremony lanes: micro, standard, high-blast-radius.

## Procedure

1. Name the kind of thing and where it will be read (CLI, file
   path, quest doc, chat). The same concept may need the kind's
   local spelling: kebab-case for slugs and files, lowercase
   words for verbs and stages.
2. Search the vocabulary above and the repo for the concept's
   existing word. If a word exists, use it, even imperfectly.
3. If no word exists, coin the shortest concrete one: a noun for
   things, a verb for moves. Two syllables beat three; a word a

===== skills/quest/SKILL.md =====
---
name: quest
description: Turn a known intent into a bp3 Quest file at docs/quests/<date>-<slug>.md with exps, acceptance checks, and human touchpoints.
---

# Quest

```text
known intent
    |
    v
 sync -> research -> names -> exps -> red chair
    |                                   |
    +-------> docs/quests/<date>-<slug>.md
                        |
                 human approves
                        |
                   governs build
```

```python
def quest(intent):
    assert known(intent)                 # fuzzy goal -> ask()
    play_back(intent)                    # iiuc, when two reads exist
    ground = research(mvp_doc, repo, prior_quests)
    names = define_terms(intent)         # spellings synced with code/data
    exps = break_down(intent, acceptance="fails today")
    attack(goal, scope, exp_order)       # red chair, recorded
    attack(source)                       # the icebox/prompt itself
    for c in concerns if c.severity in (critical, major):
        settle(c) or append_followup_exp(c)   # proof owed, not prose
    return write_quest_file(names, exps)  # governs after human approval
```

One output: a Quest file a build can run against.

## Context

Reading map for this skill: prose carries context, why, and how
we got here. Diagrams, pseudocode, and tables carry what we do.

===== skills/red-ink-quest-landing-demo/SKILL.md =====
---
name: red-ink-quest-landing-demo
description: Produce the Landing IIUC for a bp3 Quest being promoted — diligence gate first (every exp checked against its own acceptance; unsettled or improperly-done exps route to red-ink, not landing), then decision record, settled picture, demo traces, files touched, verification, and deliberately-open items.
---

# Red-Ink Quest Landing Demo

Use when a human or Controller in bp3 invokes this skill by
name (`/red-ink-quest-landing-demo`), when a Quest reaches
Quest-to-master promotion, or when asked “walk me through
what this quest became / what got done / give me the demos.”
One output: a Landing IIUC, presented in chat and, on human
approval, recorded at the Quest's landing.

Use `quest` instead to frame a new Quest. Use `using-bp3`
to operate quest state. Use `icebox` to freeze a deferred
idea. Use `red-ink` while a design is still being decided.

## The diligence gate (runs first, always)

Landing is a claim: every exp did what its `EXPERIMENT.md`
promised. Before any playback, check each exp:

| Check | Pass evidence |
|---|---|
| Design settled | no open questions in the exp; no unadopted `EXPERIMENT.red-ink.md` working copy |
| Acceptance met | every Acceptance case has `observed` evidence (run output, ledger line, test), not prose |
| Review judged the exp, not just the code | the review verdict binds the bound commit AND the exp's acceptance cases |

Any exp failing a check stops the landing. Do not present
a clean playback over a failed exp: open `/red-ink` on
that exp (working copy, owner as gate) and settle it
first. This is where the red ink happens — at landing
time, when the reviewer says an exp was not done properly,
the answer is a red-ink walk, not an apology paragraph in
the demo.

## Inputs

Read, in this order: the Quest file (and any working copy

===== skills/red-ink/SKILL.md =====
---
name: red-ink
description: "Present an existing design back to its owner in rounds until they say they are happy: copy the design first (original untouched), walk output first, then system inputs, then internals (data, state, functions, names, concepts); every round is Observe (teach) -> Orient (what matters) -> Decide (one detailed question, the owner's call) -> Act (one settled edit to the copy), closing with an IIUC playback and, at the end, a red chair of the settled design plus a decision record and proposed experiments (critical/major red-chair concerns appended as experiments). Use for Socratic design review of an existing artifact. Not for one-shot adversarial review (red-chair), authoring a new design (design), or interviewing an unclear request (ask, grill)."
---

# Red ink

```text
existing artifact
    |
    +-- copy --> working copy        (original stays untouched)
                     |
    walk:  output -> inputs -> data -> state -> fn -> names -> concepts
                     |
    per slice: Observe -> Orient -> Decide -> Act -> IIUC
                     |
    owner: "happy / settle"
                     v
    decision record + proposed experiments
```

```python
def red_ink(artifact, expert=None):
    copy = duplicate(artifact)           # original never edited
    icebox_hits = search_icebox(artifact)  # where we want to get to
    for slice in [output, inputs, data, state, fn, names, concepts]:
        teach(slice)                     # Observe
        orient(slice)                    # the 2-4 things that matter
        decision = ask_one(owner)        # Decide: options + recommendation
        apply_one_edit(copy, decision)   # Act
        playback()                       # IIUC: settled / changed / open
        if owner.happy:
            break
    chair = red_chair(copy)              # closing pass on the settled design
    exps = experiments + [concern_exp(c) for c in chair
                          if c.severity in (critical, major)]
    return exit_pack(decisions, open_questions, exps, chair)
```

## Context

